      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Capparis ovata Desf. (Capparaceae) grows widely in Turkey. Flower buds and fruits of the plant are used in folk medicine for their analgesic, antirheumatismal, and diuretic effects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This study evaluated the possible antinociceptive effect of the methanol extract of C. ovata (CME) in mice."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS",
          "content": "The antinociceptive effect of methanol extract, prepared with the C. ovata flower buds, was studied at the doses of 50, 100, and 200 mg/kg (i.p.) using tail-immersion, hot-plate, and writhing tests in mice. Morphine sulfate (5 mg/kg; i.p.) and dipyrone (100 mg/kg; i.p.) were used as reference analgesic agents. Naloxone (5 mg/kg; i.p.) was also tested."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "It was observed that the C. ovata extract had a significant antinociceptive effect in these tests. In the hot-plate and tail-immersion test results, the doses of 50, 100, and 200 mg/kg increased the percentage of the maximum possible effect (MPE%) value for nociception significantly according to the control value (P\u2009<\u20090.001). All doses of the extract decreased the number of acetic acid-induced abdominal constrictions in mice when compared with control group (P\u2009<\u20090.001). These effects were inhibited by pretreatment with naloxone."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Based on the results obtained, it can be concluded that CME is a potentially antinociceptive agent which acts as both at the peripheral and central levels."
        }
      ]},
